.TH tuvalidate n 0.2 tclutils
.SH tclutils::tuvalidate
.PP
Small format/type validation predicates. Each returns a boolean \fB1\fR/\fB0\fR and never throws on the value being checked (only on bad \fIarguments\fR, e.g. a non\-integer bound). Pure Tcl, no dependencies.
.CS
tuvalidate::email   $s            ;# pragmatic email shape
tuvalidate::url     $s            ;# http/https URL
tuvalidate::ipv4    $s            ;# dotted quad, octets 0..255, no leading zeros
tuvalidate::port    $s            ;# decimal digits only, value 1..65535
tuvalidate::alpha   $s            ;# ^[A-Za-z]+$   (ASCII)
tuvalidate::alnum   $s            ;# ^[A-Za-z0-9]+$
tuvalidate::numeric $s            ;# [string is double -strict]
tuvalidate::integer $s            ;# [string is integer -strict]
tuvalidate::length  $s $min $max  ;# min <= length <= max
tuvalidate::pattern $s $regex     ;# regexp match
tuvalidate::inList  $s $list      ;# membership
.CE
.PP
These are pragmatic checks, not full RFC validators \-\- \fBemail\fR/\fBurl\fR accept the common shapes and reject the obviously broken ones. For Unicode letter classes use the core \fB[string is alpha]\fR; \fBalpha\fR/\fBalnum\fR here are ASCII\-only by design.
.PP
\fBipv4\fR is strict like \fBinet_pton\fR: exactly four octets of ASCII digits, each \fB0\fR..\fB255\fR and without leading zeros (\fB010.0.0.1\fR gives 0, \fB0.0.0.0\fR gives 1), no surrounding whitespace.
.PP
\fBport\fR accepts ASCII decimal digits only \-\- no sign, no whitespace, no \fB0x\fR prefix. Leading zeros are allowed and read as decimal (\fB080\fR is port 80), the value must be \fB1\fR..\fB65535\fR. \fB0x50\fR and \fB" 80"\fR give 0. The result is the same under Tcl 8.6 and 9.
.PP
Bad arguments raise \fB{TCLUTILS TUVALIDATE ARG}\fR (length) or \fB{TCLUTILS TUVALIDATE REGEX}\fR (pattern).
